v0.15.0:
    - Added: the repository is ready to be published on GitHub (j5guy/waypoints-gateway), following PUBLISHING_RUNBOOK.md. New: README.md (what it is, the one-line install, what to expect, update and remove, support), LICENSE (MIT, the same as the other apps), .github/FUNDING.yml (copied from the other apps), .gitignore (runtime state, keys and certificates are never committed), .gitleaks.toml, and the gates: .githooks/pre-commit (project identity, blocked file types, secrets, private denylist), .githooks/pre-push and scripts/publish-gate.sh (the same checks on the whole history, plus unexpected refs), and .githooks/blocked-paths.regex. Turn the hooks on once per clone with: git config core.hooksPath .githooks
    - Added: prod.yml publishes to GitHub. In order: the publish gate runs before anything is built or pushed; the image is built and pushed to GHCR; master and tags are pushed to GitHub fast-forward only (no --force); a fresh clone of GitHub is checked with the gate again; a GitHub release is created for the version if there is none. New secrets: REMOTE_REPO_TOKEN (a secret name cannot start with GITHUB_) and PUBLISH_DENYLIST. The runner needs gitleaks.
    - Fixed (also in the runbook's script): the gate did not look at a commit message when the commit changed no file outside .gitea and LICENSE (an empty commit, for example), so a denylisted word there passed. Every commit message and name is now scanned on its own. 14 checks on throwaway repositories cover the gate: identity (author and committer), the secret scanner, the denylist in files, in removed history and in messages, no denylist or an empty one, blocked file types in history, and unexpected refs.
    - Found, not changed: all 17 existing commits in this repository use a personal email address, which the gate refuses and which would be published. The history has to be rewritten to the project identity (runbook Stage 1) before the first push.
    - Upgrade: none for the Gateway itself.

v0.14.1:
    - Changed: the top bar is laid out like the other Waypoints apps. The Gateway's name is on the left, the menu (Home, Certificates, Updates, Shared settings, Settings) is on the right with Sign out at the far right, and the version number is gone from the bar (it is in the footer).
    - Changed: Follow us on X is on its own line in the footer (public/footer.js), below the version, What's new and Docs line.
    - Changed: the shortcuts under the app cards are pill buttons, not text links (the .pill and .pills styles in public/style.css). The first pill shows the update state with a coloured dot (green when everything is up to date, amber when something has an update) and opens the Updates page; the others are the certificate steps, hide or show the apps you have not installed, Add an app, and Look for apps installed outside the Gateway.
    - Upgrade: none.

v0.14.0:
    - Added: an encrypted setup address. The Gateway also serves its page on https port 8443 (lib/tls.js; HTTPS_PORT, 0 turns it off) with a self-signed certificate it makes itself on first start (openssl, now in the image), so the password is not sent in plain text while setting up. The certificate names localhost, waypoints-gateway and the computer's address (GATEWAY_IP, which install.sh fills in) and is remade when that address changes or it has under 30 days left; the key is kept next to the secrets key. It uses neither 80 nor 443, so it cannot clash with an existing proxy. The session cookie is Secure on it. install.sh prints https://<address>:8443 first (with what the browser warning means) and the plain address as a fallback; the sign-in page points anyone on the plain address to the encrypted one. docker-compose.yml and the copy inside install.sh publish 8443 and pass GATEWAY_IP.
    - Added: use your own certificate in a domain install (lib/certs.js, POST /api/cert-check, POST /api/certificate). The Address step offers Get free certificates (Let's Encrypt) or Use a certificate I already have: paste or choose the certificate (or full chain) and the private key. The Gateway checks that it is a certificate and a key, that they belong together, that the key has no passphrase, that it has not expired (a warning under 30 days), that it is not self-signed or missing the intermediates (warnings), and that it covers every address that will use it (a wildcard covers one level); the last step checks it against the real addresses before Install unlocks. It is saved for the proxy (/data/certs, mode 0600; the Caddyfile names it for the Gateway's own address and every app, with no ACME email). The Certificates page shows what it covers and when it ends, warns when it is close, and replaces it after the same checks. Not encrypted at rest like the saved passwords, because the proxy has to read the file.
    - Added: older hardware is recognised (lib/hardware.js). MongoDB 5 and newer need ARMv8.2-A on ARM (a Raspberry Pi 4 or older does not have it; a Pi 5 does) and AVX on Intel/AMD. At install the Gateway reads the CPU (/proc/cpuinfo and the device-tree model) and, for a new app, picks mongo:4.4.18 by itself; the Check step says so and the Database version setting shows it. A 32-bit ARM system is refused with an instruction to use the 64-bit OS. When an existing install is brought in, its database version is kept (its data is in that format).
    - Added: settings during install. Each new app in the install step has a Settings section (optional): the same everyday and Advanced settings as its page (lib/settingsutil.js is now shared by both), a tickbox per shared group the app can use (Use the shared Email settings and so on; ticked when the group is set up and covers the app, disabled with an explanation when it is not set up yet), and the settings those groups manage are hidden while ticked. installer.validate checks the values like the settings page; installer.seedNewApp applies the ticked groups, makes the app part of them from then on, and applies the older MongoDB where needed.
    - Added: it is clear what cannot be set from the Gateway. Each catalog app lists insideApp notes (for example Charging: email is each person's own mail server; Balanced: bank sync is inside the app), shown under its Settings and in the install step. On an app's page a setting that a shared group also sets says so, and the page now knows which settings are shared (GET /api/state returns sharedInfo and sharedEnv).
    - Changed: shared groups report configured (something saved). A save on the Shared settings page refreshes what the other pages know.
    - Not done: the self-signed address was only exercised with a client trusting that certificate (not in a browser); the container build (openssl) and the install-time settings screens have not been run against a real Docker; choosing mongo:7 explicitly on hardware that needs the older version is overridden to the older one.
    - Upgrade: add the new 8443 port and GATEWAY_IP line (the new docker-compose.yml has them); until then the plain address works as before.

v0.13.0:
    - Added: uninstall.sh, a script that removes the Gateway safely. Three levels: no option removes the Gateway and its proxy and leaves the apps and all data; --apps also removes the apps the Gateway installed (containers only, their data stays in volumes); --data also deletes all the data (the Gateway's settings and key, the proxy's certificates and every app's volumes), copies any Gateway backups to ~/waypoints-backups-<date> first and stops if that fails, and needs "delete my data" typed. It lists exactly what it will do and asks before changing anything; --dry-run changes nothing; --yes skips the questions (for tests); --dir points at the Gateway's folder.
    - Added: it only touches what the Gateway made. The Gateway and proxy containers are found by name, the apps by their waypoints-<app> Compose project, the volumes by name (waypoints-gateway-data, waypoints-gateway-key, waypoints-caddy-data and waypoints-<app>_*). Containers kept from before an app was brought in (named ...-before-gateway-...), their volumes, and unrelated containers and volumes are never touched. The shared waypoints-proxy network is removed only when nothing still uses it.
    - Changed: scripts/check.sh also syntax-checks uninstall.sh; install.sh's closing message mentions the uninstall script.
    - Tested against a pretend Docker (27 checks: each level touches only what it should, the backup copy runs before the data volume is deleted, --dry-run changes nothing, nothing is removed when it cannot ask). Not run against a real Docker.
    - Upgrade: none.

v0.12.0:
    - Added: a Look for apps installed outside the Gateway link on the home page (under the cards). The page only looked when it opened; this looks again on demand (GET /api/adoptable now returns { apps, notes } from adopt.scan). If something can be brought in, the notice with Bring it in appears above the cards; if not, it says so.
    - Added: reasons when nothing can be brought in. scan() returns plain-language notes for things that look like one of our apps but cannot be imported: the app is found but not its MongoDB container (it must be started from the same compose file), or something named like the app is not running from the published image (for example built from source), with a pointer to install fresh and restore a backup.
    - Changed: detect() now only lists an app when its database container is found; before, an app without one was listed and then failed once Bring it in was pressed.
    - Upgrade: none.

v0.11.2:
    - Fixed: the two repository variables are now LOCAL_GITEA_HOST and LOCAL_GITEA_REPO (they were GITEA_HOST and GITEA_REPO, which Gitea refuses because it reserves names that start with GITEA_). All three workflows, their first-step messages, their header comments and the internals docs use the new names. The value of LOCAL_GITEA_HOST is the bare host name (no https:// and no git@); LOCAL_GITEA_REPO is owner/name with no .git. The workflows' own internal environment names are unchanged.
    - Upgrade: use the names LOCAL_GITEA_HOST and LOCAL_GITEA_REPO when adding the variables (replacing what 0.11.1 asked for).

v0.11.1:
    - Changed: the three workflows (prod.yml, test.yml, docs.yml) no longer contain a server address or repository path. They read two repository variables, GITEA_HOST (your Gitea's address) and GITEA_REPO (owner/name, for example allthewaypoints/gateway), from Settings > Actions > Variables, so the repository can be published without naming an internal host. Each workflow starts with a step that stops with a plain message if either variable is not set. The workflows disagreed about the repository path before (web/waypoints-gateway in two, allthewaypoints/gateway in test.yml); they now all use GITEA_REPO.
    - Upgrade: add the two variables to the repository before the next push, or the workflows stop at their first step.

v0.11.0:
    - Added: install.sh, a one-step installer (`curl -fsSL <address> | bash`, or `bash install.sh`, with `--dir <folder>` and `--app <name>`). One file: it carries its own copy of docker-compose.yml. It checks that Docker, Docker Compose and the Docker daemon are ready (each with a plain message and a stop if not), warns if port 8080 is taken, saves the compose file and a .env (the chosen app and this computer's time zone) in ~/waypoints-gateway, pulls and starts the Gateway, waits for /healthz, and prints the address to open and the 15-minute note. Running it again updates the Gateway.
    - Changed: scripts/check.sh now syntax-checks install.sh and fails if the compose file embedded in it differs from docker-compose.yml.
    - Changed: the storage check no longer probes a folder that has already failed (for example an overlap), so the page does not add a pointless "Could not look at that location".
    - Not done: install.sh has not been run (it was only syntax-checked and run with --help); it assumes the script is published at raw.githubusercontent.com/j5guy/waypoints-gateway/master/install.sh, which needs a public repository of that name.
    - Upgrade: none.

v0.10.0:
    - Changed: moving where an app's data lives is checked before anything happens. manage.checkStorage (POST /api/app/<id>/storage-check) looks at the choice without stopping the app and returns plain results (ok, info, warn, error; only an error blocks): the path is valid and not a system folder; it does not overlap another folder of this or any other app; Docker can write to it; it is empty (or, when "already holds my data" is ticked, not empty); there is enough free space (size x 1.1, with a warning below x 1.5); and, for the Database, it is not on a network drive (nfs, cifs, smb, sshfs...), which can damage MongoDB. The move runs the same checks again before it touches anything.
    - Changed: the move is safer. After copying, the copy is compared with the original (same number of files and folders) before the app is pointed at it; after the app starts it is waited for (running and healthy, 120 seconds) and, if it does not come up, the old place is put back automatically and the app restarted. The old copy is always kept.
    - Changed: the Where your data lives card shows the choice clearly. Each part of the data is a row with its current place; open it to pick Docker-managed or A folder on this computer, press Check this location to see the results, and Move my data unlocks only when the checks pass (with a final confirmation). Changing the choice discards the last check.
    - Fixed: folded sections did not show an open/close arrow (a flex summary hides the browser's own), so it was not clear they could be opened. They now have an arrow that turns when open.
    - Not done: untested end to end (the helper container probes and the health wait need a real Docker run); no button yet to delete the kept old copy.
    - Upgrade: none.

v0.9.1:
    - Changed: on an app's page the Uninstall section is always last (after Where your data lives and Your reverse proxy), so the destructive action is never in the middle of the page. The other sections stay A to Z.
