v0.9.0:
    - Added: a color for each app's card. An app's page has an Icon and color section with a color picker (and Remove the color); the card on the home page gets a colored top edge and a light tint of that color so the apps you use stand out. It is saved in state.appearance.cards (validated against the catalog by lib/appearance.js, saved through POST /api/appearance), so it is the same on every device, and works for apps that are not installed too.
    - Changed: the sections on the Settings page (Appearance, Backups, Network, Password, Saved secrets) are folded away until opened (a section() helper), with a one-line note in the header where useful (for example whether the encryption key is kept apart).
    - Changed: inside an app's Settings, every group is folded away and the groups are listed A to Z; in Advanced settings the listed groups, Anything else, Memory and Version are all folded and sorted together.
    - Changed: the sections on every page are listed A to Z: Settings (Appearance, Backups, Network, Password, Saved secrets), Updates (Automatic updates, Updates), Shared settings (Automatic backups, Email, Sign in with your company directory (LDAP), Single sign-on (OIDC), Time zone), an app's page (Checks, Icon and color, Info, Log, Settings, Uninstall, Where your data lives, Your reverse proxy), Where your data lives (Backups, Database, ...) and the device steps on Certificates (Android, iPhone or iPad, Linux, Mac, Windows). The home page keeps your apps first, then the ones not installed, each A to Z.
    - Upgrade: none.

v0.8.0:
    - Added: Settings > Appearance, like the other Waypoints apps (lib/appearance.js, POST /api/appearance, public/theme.js). Theme (match the device, light or dark); for each of the light and dark themes the text, secondary text, background, accent, menu text and menu background colors, each with a Reset; font (standard, serif, monospace, rounded); text size; spacing (comfortable or compact); reduce motion. Changes preview live and Save keeps them in state.appearance, so they are the same on every device and also theme the sign-in and What's new pages. theme.js is loaded in the head with the last appearance cached in the browser, so there is no flash of the wrong colors. The accent button text switches between black and white for contrast.
    - Added: app icons. Each app has a stock icon (public/icons/<id>.svg, taken from the apps' own icons) shown on its card in place of the colored letter; an app's page has an Icon section to upload your own (PNG, JPEG, WebP, GIF or SVG up to 512 KB) or go back to the stock one (lib/icons.js, GET /icons/<id>, POST /api/app-icon/<id> and /reset). Uploads are identified by their first bytes, SVGs with scripts or embedded content are refused, and icons are served with a policy that stops an SVG running anything. A custom icon is kept in /data/icons.
    - Fixed: cards in a row were not lined up. The top of a card now takes the spare height (descriptions are limited to three lines), so the status line (Running, Up to date) and the buttons sit at the same height on every card.
    - Changed: style.css uses variables for the menu colors, font, text size and spacing; the dark theme can be forced as well as followed.
    - Upgrade: none. Existing installs look as before until Appearance is changed.

v0.7.1:
    - Changed: on the Shared settings page every group (Email, LDAP, single sign-on, automatic backups, time zone) is a collapsed section. Its header shows the group name and where it is applied (all apps, a number of apps, or not applied yet); opening it shows the fields, the Apply to choices and Save and apply. Markup is a `details.shared-group` in public/app.js sharedView with styles in public/style.css.
    - Upgrade: none.

v0.7.0:
    - Added: saved secrets are encrypted at rest (lib/secrets.js). Passwords, keys and tokens saved in state.json (settings whose catalog type is secret or whose name looks like a secret, the Advanced free-form rows with such a name, and the secret fields of Shared settings) are written as `enc:v1:` AES-256-GCM tokens and are plain only in memory (state.read decrypts, state.write encrypts).
    - Added: the key is generated automatically in master.key in its own volume, waypoints-gateway-key, mounted at /key (new gateway-key volume in docker-compose.yml), apart from the data volume. When /key is not a mount point (an older compose file) the key stays in the data volume, the Settings page says so, and the key is moved across by itself once the volume is added. A secret that cannot be decrypted (key lost or changed) reads as empty and is logged.
    - Changed: the apps' Compose override files no longer contain secrets. A secret is written as a `${NAME}` placeholder and installer.composeEnv passes the real value to Docker Compose from memory (secrets.composeSecrets), so every compose command the Gateway runs already carries it.
    - Added: at start the Gateway encrypts secrets an older version saved in plain text and rewrites every override file with placeholders (state.migrate in server.js). GET /api/state now also returns whether the key is separate; the Settings page has a Saved secrets card.
    - Not done: apps still receive their secrets as environment variables (visible with docker inspect), and root or the Docker socket reaches both the key and the data; backups still contain each app's own secrets volume unencrypted; no password-derived key and no encrypted backups yet.
    - Upgrade: add the gateway-key volume (the new docker-compose.yml has it) so the key is kept apart; without it the Gateway still works and encrypts, with the key beside the data. Do not delete waypoints-gateway-key: without it saved secrets must be entered again.

v0.6.0:
    - Changed: settings are now real lists, not a free-form box. Each catalog app declares `settings` with a `level` (basic or advanced), group, label, help, type (text, number, email, bool, secret, choice), default and, for choices, options. The app page shows the basic ones as fields, then a collapsed Advanced settings section with a "for advanced users" warning, the listed advanced options, an "Anything else" name/value list, a pinned version and a memory limit. One Save and restart button; the page says the app restarts. Lists written from each app's environment-variable docs: Fond (sign-up, email, emergency access, backups), Loadout (time zone, email, limits, S3 media, Tailscale, settings storage, connection string), Balanced (display, sign-up, email, private-network access, connection string) and Charging (LDAP and SSO sign-in, trust options).
    - Changed: listed settings and free-form rows are saved in state (`apps.<id>.settings`, `apps.<id>.extra`) and written to the Compose override (`lib/compose.js writeOverride(id, appState)`); settings aimed at Compose itself (`target: "compose"`, the database image) stay with the app's environment. `POST /api/app/<id>/settings` takes `{ values, extra }`; `/advanced` is removed. Empty or default values are removed instead of saved, secrets are only replaced when typed and can be cleared with a link, and free-form rows whose name looks like a secret are hidden on screen.
    - Added: Where your data lives. Each app's page has a Storage card: every data location is shown as Docker-managed or as a folder, with a Change button. Moving stops the app, refuses a destination folder that already has files (unless "This folder already holds my data" is ticked), copies with `cp -a` in a helper container, saves the new place, starts the app and keeps the old copy. The catalog compose files now read `${..._HOST_DIR:-volume}` for every volume (`storage` in app.json lists them). Folder paths are validated (`manage.cleanFolder`). A wipe-uninstall no longer implies folders are deleted: they are left in place and named; backups read from the folder instead of the volume.
    - Added: bring in an install from before the Gateway (`lib/adopt.js`, `GET /api/adoptable`, `POST /api/adopt`). The home page shows a banner when a container using a Waypoints image that the Gateway does not manage is found. Bringing it in takes an optional backup first (default on, taken while the old install runs, via `uninstall.backup` with the old containers' mounts), stops the old app and database, renames them `<name>-before-gateway-<date>` (restart policy off) so nothing is deleted, copies every data location into the Gateway's volumes, imports its settings (listed ones, plus every other environment variable as "anything else" rows, including a custom database name and hand-set secrets), starts it under the Gateway and adds its address to the proxy. Any failure puts the old install back. Refused when the old install uses its own database server or login.
    - Added: Shared settings (menu item, lib/shared.js, GET /api/shared, POST /api/shared/<group>, manage.saveShared). Email, LDAP, single sign-on, automatic backups and time zone are set once and carried to all apps that use them (including apps installed later) or the ticked ones. MAP translates each shared field to every app's own variable names (Fond SMTP_FROM, Loadout and Balanced MAIL_FROM, Loadout LDAP through AUTH_MODE and ldapURL..., Balanced and Charging LDAP_*, Charging OIDC_*, Fond BACKUP_*, TZ and DEFAULT_TIMEZONE). The apps' override files are rewritten and only apps whose settings changed are restarted; the page is the source of truth for the apps it is applied to, so an empty field is removed from them. installer.apply seeds a newly installed app (state.shared, seedNew) and keeps shared settings when setup is run again.
    - Changed: automatic updates are per app. state.updates is { apps, hour, day, backup, last } (replacing the single autoUpdate switch, which is migrated to "on" for every app). The Updates page has an Automatic updates card (apps, every day or one weekday, hour, back up first) and each app's page has its own switch (POST /api/auto-update). The scheduler checks every 15 minutes, runs at the chosen hour in the Gateway's time zone (new TZ line in docker-compose.yml), marks each app as checked for the day before acting, and skips an app whose backup fails.
    - Fixed: GET /api/state sent the whole saved state to the browser, which would have included shared and per-app secrets. It now sends only addresses, ports and the sign-up fields (publicState in server.js).
    - Added: Hide apps I haven't installed on the home page (remembered in this browser; with nothing installed they always show). An Add an app link appears when they are hidden.
    - Not done: untested end to end (storage move, adoption and backup paths all need a run); adoption only finds installs that use the published image and a MongoDB container in the same Compose project; no button yet to remove the kept old containers (docs give the command).
    - Upgrade: none from 0.5.0 for apps installed by the Gateway: their data stays in the same Docker volumes. Settings saved in 0.3.0 to 0.5.0 builds that predate this list are not migrated.

v0.5.0:
    - Added: uninstall an app from its page (lib/uninstall.js, POST /api/app/<id>/uninstall). Two choices: remove it and keep its data (docker compose down; the named volumes stay, so installing the app again reattaches them) or remove it and delete its data (down -v, only after typing the app's name). Either can take a backup first.
    - Added: backups. The database is dumped with mongodump inside the app's mongo container and each other project volume is tarred by a helper container (docker:27-cli), then packed into one .tar.gz in /data/exports (mode 0600) with a README. If any step fails nothing is removed. Backups are listed, downloaded and deleted on the Settings page (GET /api/exports, GET /api/exports/<name>, POST /api/exports/<name>/delete). Restoring from a backup file is manual (mongorestore plus tar); reinstalling after Keep my data is the supported way back.
    - Added: runToFile in lib/run.js; the app page's Uninstall card and the uninstall view; danger button styles.
    - Not done: untested end to end (mongodump in the stock mongo image, the volume tar helper); no one-click restore from a backup file; the Docker images are left in place after an uninstall.
    - Upgrade: none.

v0.4.0:
    - Added: a What's new page at /whats-new (public/whats-new.html, public/whatsnew.js, release data in lib/whatsNew.js, served by GET /api/whats-new). It needs no sign-in, like the other Waypoints apps, and shows the highlights of the latest three releases and the full notes of every release.
    - Added: a footer on every page (public/footer.js, GET /api/site) with the version, What's new, Docs (https://support.allthewaypoints.com/docs/gateway-waypoints/overview) and Follow us on X (https://x.com/allthewaypoints; X_URL overrides it, an empty X_URL hides it; lib/community.js).
    - Added: scripts/check.sh now fails CI unless package.json, the newest RELEASE_NOTES heading and the newest lib/whatsNew.js entry name the same version, so a change cannot ship without release notes and a version bump. It also syntax-checks the new files.
    - Upgrade: none. The docs link assumes a gateway-waypoints docs space.

v0.3.0:
    - Changed: sign-in is now a password the first visitor creates (replacing the setup code that had to be read from the container log). It is stored as a scrypt hash in auth.json, only creatable in the first 15 minutes after the container starts, with the same lockout. reset-password.js clears it (docker exec, then restart). The login view, the change-password card and the /api/auth, /api/claim and /api/password routes are new.
    - Added: the gateway's own HTTPS address (https://gateway.<base name>) in built-in mode. Setup attaches waypoints-gateway to the proxy network and adds a Caddy site for it; the session cookie is Secure when the request came through HTTPS.
    - Added: an app page per app (Manage): status, Start/Restart/Stop, plain-language Checks (app, health, database, proxy, HTTPS address, each with a one-sentence fix), Settings, the log (last 200 lines) and Info. Proxy snippets for own-proxy setups moved here.
    - Added: settings for Fond (sign-up allowlist, email, emergency-access days, backups) and Loadout (email, time zone), declared in catalog app.json and read by the compose files. Saving restarts the app. Secrets are never sent back to the browser.
    - Added: an Advanced box on each app's page: extra environment variables (any name and value, so any setting the app supports can be changed from the UI), a pinned version (the app stops getting update offers until cleared) and a memory limit. Saved as a Compose override in /data/overrides/<id>.yml (lib/compose.js) that every install, update and restart now includes. Values there are not masked, so passwords belong in a secret-type setting.
    - Changed: a top menu (Home, Certificates, Updates, Settings). The home page is the app cards followed by a one-line summary (updates, and a link to the certificate steps on a home network). The certificate steps, the Updates box and the network and password settings each have their own page; the menu's Updates item shows a count of waiting updates.
    - Added: update the Gateway from its own page. The Updates box shows whether the Gateway is up to date, or an Update the Gateway button; a short-lived helper container pulls the new image and recreates the gateway from the Compose file it was started with, and the page reconnects when the version changes. Falls back to showing the command when that is not possible (Docker Desktop on Windows or macOS). /api/auth now includes the version.
    - Changed: the home page is a portal. A card per app: installed apps in full color with a status dot, an Up to date or Update available badge, an Open button and (when needed) Update; clicking the card opens that app's page. Apps that are not installed are grayed-out cards, and clicking one starts adding it. The app page also shows the update status with an Update now button. The separate Add an app button is gone (the cards replace it).
    - Fixed: going back into setup ticked every installed app again. Installed apps are now marked Installed and cannot be ticked or installed twice. A new Add an app button skips the network questions and keeps the existing address setup; the old button is now Change network settings. Setup also skips an installed app whose address and settings have not changed, so adding an app no longer pulls and restarts the others.
    - Changed: re-running setup keeps settings saved on an app's page (it used to rebuild them from the wizard fields alone). The dashboard cards are now Open, Manage, Update.
    - Changed: the Test button moved into Checks. docker-compose.yml, docs and workflows no longer mention the setup code.
    - Not done: untested end to end; settings are stored unencrypted in state.json (mode 0600) and show in docker inspect; Balanced and Charging have no settings schema yet; port 8080 is still published over plain HTTP.
    - Upgrade: from 0.2.0 an existing gateway has no password. Restart it and open the page within 15 minutes to create one.

v0.2.0:
    - Added: one-click updates. After setup the page shows Update available or Up to date for each app (by comparing the local image digest with the registry's), an Update button per app, Update all, and a status line. Updating pulls the newest image and recreates the container; data is in volumes and is kept. A failed download changes nothing. The built-in Caddy proxy is refreshed too. See lib/updates.js and installer.updateApps.
    - Added: optional automatic updates (off by default): every 6 hours, at most once a day, only while the gateway is running.
    - Added: the gateway notices when its own image is newer and shows the command to update it (it cannot replace itself).
    - Added: Balanced Waypoints and Charging Waypoints in the catalog (from their standalone composes: WEB_PROTOCOL=https, TRUST_PROXY=1, ADMIN_EMAIL asked in the wizard). Catalog entries now declare their image.
    - Added: PRESELECT environment variable (in docker-compose.yml) to tick an app by default; each product's install page uses it.
    - Changed: docs no longer tell people to stop the gateway after setup. It is meant to run all the time; docs-admin/site/security.md lists the hardening still to do for that (HTTPS address for the gateway, an administrator password in place of the setup code).
    - Not done: untested end to end; the digest check needs the GHCR packages to be public; Balanced and Charging entries are built from their dist-example files and not checked against a running install.
    - Upgrade: none from 0.1.0 beyond pulling the new image.

v0.1.0:
    - Added: the Waypoints Gateway, a click-through setup that gives Waypoints apps HTTPS. One container with a web page (port 8080), unlocked by a one-time setup code from the container log.
    - Added: built-in proxy. The gateway installs Caddy on a shared waypoints-proxy network and writes its Caddyfile. Home network mode uses Caddy's local CA (devices trust the downloadable root certificate once) with sslip.io names so no local DNS is needed; domain mode gets Let's Encrypt certificates.
    - Added: own-proxy mode. The gateway checks Docker and ports 80/443 (and looks for a proxy container or a program answering on the host), recommends built-in or own proxy, and in own-proxy mode publishes one port per app and shows paste-ready settings for nginx, Apache, Traefik, Caddy and Nginx Proxy Manager, plus a Test button.
    - Added: catalog with Fond Waypoints and Loadout Waypoints. Each app is a folder in catalog/ (app.json, compose.yml, own-proxy.yml); adding another needs no code.
    - Not done: untested end to end (written without running Docker); the first CI run is the first time the image is built; no update or remove from the page; Balanced, Charging, Scored, Stocked and Answered are not in the catalog.
    - Upgrade: new project, nothing to migrate. Fond and Loadout installed by hand keep their own volumes; the gateway installs separate copies under the waypoints-<app> compose projects.
